1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,950
Your sweat does not smell.

2
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:04,720
Fresh out of the gland, what comes out

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,410
under your arms has
almost no smell at all.

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,970
What you smell is what
happens to it afterwards.

5
00:00:12,070 --> 00:00:13,880
And your body does not make it.

6
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:16,290
You have two kinds of sweat gland.

7
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:20,230
The ones spread over your whole
skin are there to cool you down.

8
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:26,540
That sweat is almost entirely water.
It is not the problem. It never was.

9
00:00:27,343 --> 00:00:32,073
The other kind sits in your armpits and
your groin. They switch on at puberty.

10
00:00:33,303 --> 00:00:37,533
And what they push out is a large,
heavy molecule with no smell whatsoever.

11
00:00:38,713 --> 00:00:43,123
It is a package. A sulphur atom,
wrapped up so that it cannot fly.

12
00:00:44,303 --> 00:00:46,013
And you cannot smell a package.

13
00:00:46,783 --> 00:00:49,973
A smell has to reach your nose,
which means it has to travel.

14
00:00:51,013 --> 00:00:52,903
Then a bacterium finds it.

15
00:00:53,643 --> 00:00:55,353
It lives all over your skin.

16
00:00:56,103 --> 00:00:57,833
Here it has something to work on.

17
00:00:57,963 --> 00:00:59,993
And it carries the right tools.

18
00:01:00,911 --> 00:01:02,841
One enzyme trims the wrapping.

19
00:01:03,651 --> 00:01:06,921
A second enzyme cuts the bond
holding the sulphur in place.

20
00:01:08,051 --> 00:01:10,361
And the thing that
floats away is the smell.

21
00:01:11,481 --> 00:01:13,271
So your body makes a component.

22
00:01:14,081 --> 00:01:16,551
The bacteria on your skin finish the job.

23
00:01:17,391 --> 00:01:20,081
You are not the factory.
You are the supplier.

24
00:01:22,661 --> 00:01:24,691
The finished article travels well.

25
00:01:25,641 --> 00:01:28,871
Some odour molecules register
at a few parts per billion.

26
00:01:30,101 --> 00:01:31,911
That is a ratio, not an amount.

27
00:01:32,781 --> 00:01:36,631
A few molecules in every billion, and
your brain says: something smells.

28
00:01:37,791 --> 00:01:40,261
Hold on to that. We are coming back to it.

29
00:01:42,951 --> 00:01:44,721
That leaves an awkward question.

30
00:01:45,731 --> 00:01:47,611
Why would a body run
a process whose entire

31
00:01:47,691 --> 00:01:50,321
output is thrown into the surrounding air?

32
00:01:51,391 --> 00:01:55,801
Because smell is older than soap.
Older than cities. Older than words.

33
00:01:56,671 --> 00:02:01,161
For most of life on Earth it was
never decoration. It was the message.

34
00:02:02,201 --> 00:02:05,531
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

35
00:02:06,301 --> 00:02:07,771
It really helps the channel.

36
00:02:09,491 --> 00:02:11,281
Watch almost any other animal.

37
00:02:11,851 --> 00:02:16,221
A smell tells it who you are. Where
you are. Which group you are from.

38
00:02:16,931 --> 00:02:21,241
Whether you are ready to mate. Whether
that is food. Whether you are ill.

39
00:02:21,999 --> 00:02:24,009
That last one sounds like a stretch.

40
00:02:25,139 --> 00:02:26,749
It is not a stretch at all.

41
00:02:28,999 --> 00:02:30,999
A Scottish nurse called Joy Milne noticed

42
00:02:31,079 --> 00:02:33,469
that her husband had
begun to smell different.

43
00:02:34,769 --> 00:02:35,969
Woody.

44
00:02:36,444 --> 00:02:37,989
Musky.

45
00:02:38,439 --> 00:02:40,709
She mentioned it. Nothing came of it.

46
00:02:41,619 --> 00:02:44,889
Twelve years later he was
diagnosed with Parkinson's.

47
00:02:45,809 --> 00:02:47,689
She only made the
connection at a meeting of

48
00:02:47,769 --> 00:02:50,479
patients, where the whole
room smelled of him.

49
00:02:51,579 --> 00:02:54,829
So researchers tested her. Twelve shirts.

50
00:02:55,479 --> 00:02:58,789
Six worn by patients, six by
people without the disease.

51
00:02:59,949 --> 00:03:01,419
She picked out all six.

52
00:03:02,489 --> 00:03:05,239
And then she picked out a
seventh, and would not back down.

53
00:03:06,369 --> 00:03:08,819
That person did not have Parkinson's.

54
00:03:09,579 --> 00:03:11,909
They were diagnosed nine months later.

55
00:03:12,989 --> 00:03:15,739
The smell was traced to
skin oil on the upper back.

56
00:03:16,799 --> 00:03:18,599
It had been sitting there for years, on an

57
00:03:18,679 --> 00:03:21,669
ordinary shirt, and nobody
knew how to read it.

58
00:03:24,389 --> 00:03:27,779
And she is only an extreme case
of something completely ordinary.

59
00:03:29,049 --> 00:03:32,419
Every person carries a slightly
different mixture of molecules.

60
00:03:33,769 --> 00:03:35,619
Part of it is your own chemistry.

61
00:03:36,409 --> 00:03:39,799
Part of it is the particular
bacteria that happen to live on you.

62
00:03:40,729 --> 00:03:42,789
And part of it is what
you ate, where you live,

63
00:03:42,869 --> 00:03:44,899
what you are wearing,
and what you did today.

64
00:03:46,189 --> 00:03:49,539
So it is not really a
smell. It is a signature.

65
00:03:51,099 --> 00:03:53,249
And something out there is reading it.

66
00:03:54,109 --> 00:03:56,099
People reach for the word pheromone.

67
00:03:56,349 --> 00:03:58,899
In humans, not one has ever been found.

68
00:03:59,709 --> 00:04:01,439
A dog does not need one.

69
00:04:02,289 --> 00:04:04,729
Four dogs in Belfast were
given breath and sweat

70
00:04:04,809 --> 00:04:07,959
from one person, calm,
and then under pressure.

71
00:04:09,179 --> 00:04:10,784
They found the stressed
sample in ninety-four

72
00:04:10,864 --> 00:04:13,949
per cent of seven hundred
and twenty trials.

73
00:04:15,069 --> 00:04:16,719
And it goes further than detection.

74
00:04:17,669 --> 00:04:21,859
Human fear sweat raises a dog's heart
rate and sends it looking for its owner.

75
00:04:22,929 --> 00:04:24,319
It never saw a face.

76
00:04:25,239 --> 00:04:27,289
To the dog, none of that is a failing.

77
00:04:27,959 --> 00:04:30,029
It is simply what today smells like.

78
00:04:32,349 --> 00:04:34,119
And this is not a human trick.

79
00:04:34,629 --> 00:04:36,379
It is not even an animal trick.

80
00:04:37,489 --> 00:04:41,359
Cut the leaves of a sagebrush and
it releases chemicals into the air.

81
00:04:42,489 --> 00:04:45,269
A wild tobacco plant
growing beside it then takes

82
00:04:45,349 --> 00:04:48,799
measurably less damage from
grasshoppers and caterpillars.

83
00:04:49,859 --> 00:04:51,059
It was warned.

84
00:04:51,589 --> 00:04:54,159
No nerves. No brain. No nose.

85
00:04:54,409 --> 00:04:57,999
Just a molecule leaving one
organism and altering another.

86
00:04:58,949 --> 00:05:01,709
The range is tiny. Under
fifteen centimetres.

87
00:05:01,909 --> 00:05:03,659
It is a whisper, not a broadcast.

88
00:05:04,783 --> 00:05:08,873
But it is communication. It
simply has no words in it.

89
00:05:11,443 --> 00:05:13,633
So why does any of this disgust us?

90
00:05:14,603 --> 00:05:16,633
Because a molecule carries no opinion.

91
00:05:17,443 --> 00:05:19,593
Nothing inside it means "revolting".

92
00:05:20,283 --> 00:05:22,013
That part is added by you.

93
00:05:22,693 --> 00:05:24,903
And usually it is added for good reason.

94
00:05:26,023 --> 00:05:27,269
Rot.

95
00:05:27,719 --> 00:05:28,919
Waste.

96
00:05:29,586 --> 00:05:30,786
Infection.

97
00:05:31,474 --> 00:05:35,624
If something smells like it is decaying,
not eating it is excellent advice.

98
00:05:36,914 --> 00:05:41,604
Disgust is a warning system, and it has
kept an enormous number of people alive.

99
00:05:42,704 --> 00:05:44,814
Then something happened to that system.

100
00:05:47,864 --> 00:05:50,974
We took the warning system and
turned it into a rule of manners.

101
00:05:52,324 --> 00:05:57,014
And unlike most rules of manners, we know
who wrote this one. We know the year.

102
00:05:58,114 --> 00:06:01,054
In 1919 an advertising
agency had an awkward

103
00:06:01,134 --> 00:06:04,344
client: a sweat-blocking
liquid called Odorono.

104
00:06:05,677 --> 00:06:10,707
Sales were flat. A copywriter called
James Webb Young went to find out why.

105
00:06:11,837 --> 00:06:13,387
The answer was inconvenient.

106
00:06:13,797 --> 00:06:16,327
Two women in three did not
think it was about them.

107
00:06:17,427 --> 00:06:20,477
So he stopped selling the liquid
and started selling the problem.

108
00:06:21,667 --> 00:06:24,187
The advertisement ran
in Ladies' Home Journal.

109
00:06:24,267 --> 00:06:26,757
"Within the Curve of a Woman's Arm."

110
00:06:27,787 --> 00:06:29,437
Underneath, a subtitle.

111
00:06:30,127 --> 00:06:32,897
"A frank discussion of a
subject too often avoided."

112
00:06:34,177 --> 00:06:36,057
Two hundred readers cancelled
their subscriptions.

113
00:06:36,137 --> 00:06:39,387
Colleagues stopped speaking to him.

114
00:06:40,187 --> 00:06:43,797
Sales rose by a hundred and
twelve percent in a single year.

115
00:06:46,297 --> 00:06:48,287
None of which invented the shame.

116
00:06:49,137 --> 00:06:51,207
People had minded the smell for centuries.

117
00:06:52,207 --> 00:06:53,407
Baths. Perfume. Soap.

118
00:06:53,487 --> 00:06:56,697
Long before anybody
had a product to shift.

119
00:06:57,427 --> 00:07:00,157
What 1919 invented was the sale.

120
00:07:01,217 --> 00:07:05,047
An old, vague worry became something
you could buy your way out of.

121
00:07:06,127 --> 00:07:10,917
And once smelling is a failure rather than
a fact, there is no obvious place to stop.

122
00:07:11,897 --> 00:07:14,887
We wash. We shave. We
deodorise. We perfume.

123
00:07:15,867 --> 00:07:18,297
We wash a shirt that has been worn once.

124
00:07:18,867 --> 00:07:21,357
We build antibacterial metals into socks.

125
00:07:22,407 --> 00:07:24,507
An enormous amount of
time and money, spent

126
00:07:24,587 --> 00:07:27,037
so that a body smells of nothing at all.

127
00:07:28,167 --> 00:07:31,077
Or, better still, of something
that came out of a bottle.

128
00:07:33,777 --> 00:07:35,307
And now remember the pump.

129
00:07:36,287 --> 00:07:38,047
The one that has to
push the package out of

130
00:07:38,127 --> 00:07:40,877
the gland before a
bacterium can get at it.

131
00:07:41,997 --> 00:07:44,877
In some people it barely
works. It is inherited.

132
00:07:44,957 --> 00:07:46,787
And it is not rare.

133
00:07:47,707 --> 00:07:50,247
Hardly any package. Hardly any smell.

134
00:07:50,327 --> 00:07:51,717
Nothing to do with washing.

135
00:07:52,539 --> 00:07:57,269
Across much of East Asia that is not
the exception. It is the majority.

136
00:08:00,349 --> 00:08:01,779
So here is the test.

137
00:08:03,298 --> 00:08:05,758
If the smell itself were
the problem, a place where

138
00:08:05,838 --> 00:08:08,428
most people hardly make it
would not need the cure.

139
00:08:09,588 --> 00:08:12,258
Japan has a deodorant industry anyway.

140
00:08:13,298 --> 00:08:16,098
And Japanese has a word for
offending somebody with your smell.

141
00:08:16,178 --> 00:08:19,988
Sumehara. Smell harassment.

142
00:08:21,138 --> 00:08:23,988
The pump behind all this
comes down to a single gene.

143
00:08:24,258 --> 00:08:26,888
And that gene has a story of its own.

144
00:08:27,978 --> 00:08:29,548
That one is its own video.

145
00:08:30,258 --> 00:08:33,008
If it is up already, there
is a link above and below.

146
00:08:35,648 --> 00:08:40,238
Nobody's chemistry changed
in 1919. The rule did.

147
00:08:41,058 --> 00:08:44,868
And the rule outran the
biology. Rules do that.

148
00:08:47,608 --> 00:08:49,318
Think back to being very small.

149
00:08:50,288 --> 00:08:52,138
Things smelled the way they smelled.

150
00:08:52,768 --> 00:08:54,378
None of it was an accusation.

151
00:08:55,478 --> 00:08:57,398
Then you were taught. That one is lovely.

152
00:08:57,478 --> 00:09:00,068
That one is not. Go and have a shower.

153
00:09:02,195 --> 00:09:03,765
Now, hygiene matters.

154
00:09:04,575 --> 00:09:06,825
Nobody here is arguing against washing.

155
00:09:07,805 --> 00:09:11,115
But two things got welded together
that were never the same thing.

156
00:09:12,265 --> 00:09:14,695
Something can smell
without being dangerous.

157
00:09:15,775 --> 00:09:18,905
And something can be dangerous
and smell of nothing at all.

158
00:09:19,885 --> 00:09:22,075
Carbon monoxide has no smell.

159
00:09:22,725 --> 00:09:24,315
It kills people in their sleep.

160
00:09:25,275 --> 00:09:27,665
None of which means the
rule is simply wrong.

161
00:09:28,685 --> 00:09:32,615
Nobody wants to sit next to somebody
who has not washed in three weeks.

162
00:09:33,595 --> 00:09:37,445
But there is a great deal of room between
that, and treating a body as a fault.

163
00:09:40,105 --> 00:09:45,215
Smell is information. What we decided
it means is another matter entirely.

164
00:09:46,335 --> 00:09:47,925
Now go back to the molecules.

165
00:09:50,525 --> 00:09:53,795
A few parts in every billion,
drifting through ordinary air.

166
00:09:54,885 --> 00:09:55,965
Enough to make you hungry.

167
00:09:56,045 --> 00:09:58,575
Enough to make you step back.

168
00:09:59,275 --> 00:10:02,605
Enough to put you in a kitchen you
have not stood in for twenty years.

169
00:10:03,785 --> 00:10:08,435
An amount of matter too small to weigh,
rearranging your opinion of a person.

170
00:10:09,625 --> 00:10:11,575
Because you are not reacting to a smell.

171
00:10:12,305 --> 00:10:13,775
You are reacting to information.

172
00:10:14,865 --> 00:10:17,125
Your body is running a
system that is older than

173
00:10:17,205 --> 00:10:20,095
language, and it never
got the message to stop.

174
00:10:21,215 --> 00:10:24,785
Underneath the soap, and the perfume,
and the freshly washed shirt,

175
00:10:25,556 --> 00:10:27,126
something is still talking.

176
00:10:29,696 --> 00:10:31,676
So the next time somebody
complains about a

177
00:10:31,756 --> 00:10:34,226
smell, remember what is actually arriving.

178
00:10:35,636 --> 00:10:38,156
Not a substance called
"bad". Just molecules.

179
00:10:38,236 --> 00:10:40,086
A few in every billion.

180
00:10:40,788 --> 00:10:45,378
Carrying information that we decided,
in 1919, we would rather not receive.

181
00:10:46,678 --> 00:10:49,798
Maybe we would all live a little
easier, with one less thing to

182
00:10:49,878 --> 00:10:53,808
worry about, if we had not turned
information into a social problem.

183
00:10:54,988 --> 00:10:56,658
We are not smelling a person.

184
00:10:57,678 --> 00:10:59,008
We are smelling molecules.
